<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edwin Hubble</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Edwin Powell Hubble</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edwin Hubble was an American astronomer, born in Marshfield, Missouri, in 1889, who helped establish extragalactic astronomy and observational cosmology. After studying at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, he joined the Mount Wilson Observatory in 1919. Using the 100-inch Hooker Telescope to find Cepheid variables, he showed in 1924 that the Andromeda and Triangulum nebulae lay far beyond the Milky Way and were galaxies in their own right. In 1929 he confirmed that a galaxy's recessional velocity increases with its distance, the relation known as Hubble's law, which implies that the universe is expanding. He died in 1953, and the Hubble Space Telescope is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edwin-hubble.jpg" alt="Edwin Hubble" width="330" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johan Hagemeyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Studio_portrait_photograph_of_Edwin_Powell_Hubble_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
